WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.093
A doctor in our hospital pulls a note from the central chart room.

2
00:00:07.093 --> 00:00:09.156
Fever at 3 a.m., gave medicine.

3
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:10.335
Which patient?

4
00:00:10.335 --> 00:00:11.096
Which ward?

5
00:00:11.096 --> 00:00:12.549
The note doesn't say.

6
00:00:12.549 --> 00:00:16.839
The nurse was standing right at the bed, so it seemed obvious.

7
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:19.664
Our log lines have the same blind spot.

8
00:00:19.664 --> 00:00:22.728
Here is a line from our lab app, hello-devops.

9
00:00:22.728 --> 00:00:24.793
Which copy of the app wrote it?

10
00:00:24.793 --> 00:00:25.925
On which machine?

11
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:27.480
Two questions, really.

12
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.396
Who even collects the lines, when the app runs on many machines?

13
00:00:31.396 --> 00:00:33.967
And how does each line learn who wrote it?

14
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:35.803
Let's take them one at a time.

15
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:39.442
Last time, everything lived on one laptop.

16
00:00:39.442 --> 00:00:47.399
Docker handed every line straight to Fluent Bit, a small program that collects logs and sends them on to Elasticsearch.

17
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.549
One machine, one collector.

18
00:00:51.549 --> 00:00:52.021
Easy.

19
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:58.940
Now hello-devops runs on Kubernetes, the system that spreads containers over many machines.

20
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.960
Picture the cluster as the hospital building.

21
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.779
Each machine, called a node, is one floor.

22
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.010
Each pod, a running copy of an app, is a bed on that floor.

23
00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.709
Three nodes, twelve pods.

24
00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:20.476
On every node, each container's output is written to a file, and all those files sit in one folder, slash var slash log slash containers.

25
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.279
Look at one file's name.

26
00:01:22.279 --> 00:01:26.490
It says who wrote it: the pod, the namespace, and the container.

27
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.320
A namespace is a named area of the cluster.

28
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.820
Keep this name in mind; it comes back.

29
00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:34.351
So who reads these folders?

30
00:01:34.351 --> 00:01:38.456
The first idea most of us have: give every bed its own clerk.

31
00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:41.265
A little shipper inside every pod.

32
00:01:41.265 --> 00:01:41.953
It works.

33
00:01:41.953 --> 00:01:44.169
Twelve pods, twelve shippers.

34
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:51.645
But grow to forty pods, and you have forty shippers, each with its own memory and its own settings to keep up to date.

35
00:01:51.645 --> 00:01:54.653
The hospital now employs more clerks than nurses.

36
00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:56.848
Fine, the opposite then.

37
00:01:56.848 --> 00:01:58.885
One clerk for the whole building.

38
00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:02.157
But a clerk can only see the folder on its own floor.

39
00:02:02.157 --> 00:02:07.404
Its window, called a hostPath volume, shows that node's own folder, and nothing else.

40
00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:09.944
Floors two and three pile up, unread.

41
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:13.564
What we really want is exactly one clerk on every floor.

42
00:02:13.564 --> 00:02:19.268
And when a new floor opens, a new clerk should appear, without anyone remembering to hire one.

43
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.765
Kubernetes has exactly that rule.

44
00:02:21.765 --> 00:02:26.020
It is called a DaemonSet: run one copy of a pod on every node.

45
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.011
The clerk here is Fluent Bit.

46
00:02:28.233 --> 00:02:29.928
So, a quick prediction.

47
00:02:29.928 --> 00:02:31.771
Three nodes, twelve pods.

48
00:02:31.771 --> 00:02:33.908
How many Fluent Bit pods run?

49
00:02:33.908 --> 00:02:35.087
Pause and guess.

50
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:38.744
Three.

51
00:02:38.744 --> 00:02:40.965
One per node, not one per pod.

52
00:02:40.965 --> 00:02:44.741
Each one reads the files of every pod on its floor.

53
00:02:44.967 --> 00:02:46.024
One small print.

54
00:02:46.024 --> 00:02:51.444
One of these nodes is the control-plane node, the one that runs Kubernetes itself.

55
00:02:51.444 --> 00:02:56.600
The lab's values include a toleration, a setting that lets Fluent Bit onto it.

56
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.583
Without it, you would get two.

57
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.434
We install it with Helm, the package installer for Kubernetes.

58
00:03:03.434 --> 00:03:06.901
The naive first try is the chart's default settings.

59
00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:07.768
Then we wait.

60
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.403
And nothing arrives.

61
00:03:09.403 --> 00:03:17.677
The default output sends to a host named elasticsearch-master, which only exists if you installed Elastic's old chart.

62
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.927
Point it at the right place, and there's a second problem.

63
00:03:21.927 --> 00:03:30.884
The default doesn't set suppress_type_name, so Elasticsearch 8 and 9 answer with HTTP 400 and unknown parameter, underscore type.

64
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.162
With unlimited retries, Fluent Bit keeps trying forever, quietly.

65
00:03:35.162 --> 00:03:40.287
A clerk posting letters to a building that was never built, and never complaining.

66
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:46.974
So the point really is: before you open Kibana and stare at an empty page, read one shipper's own log.

67
00:03:46.974 --> 00:03:49.068
It tells you why nothing arrived.

68
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.624
The lab's values file sets the output itself.

69
00:03:52.624 --> 00:03:54.028
Now the lines flow.

70
00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:55.468
They arrive.

71
00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:59.225
But look at one: still a note with no ward and no bed.

72
00:03:59.225 --> 00:04:01.103
Back to our first question.

73
00:04:01.103 --> 00:04:02.425
Which pod wrote it?

74
00:04:04.233 --> 00:04:05.696
Remember the file name?

75
00:04:05.696 --> 00:04:11.991
The clerk reads the line from that file, so the pod, the namespace and the container come for free.

76
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:17.199
The kubernetes filter, a step inside Fluent Bit, stamps them onto the record.

77
00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.827
Labels are different.

78
00:04:18.827 --> 00:04:24.737
They are short name and value tags that people attach to a pod, like app: hello-devops.

79
00:04:24.737 --> 00:04:26.775
They are not in the file name.

80
00:04:26.775 --> 00:04:30.376
So the clerk asks the front desk, the Kubernetes API.

81
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:36.822
And it keeps the answer in memory, a cache, so it doesn't run to the desk again for every single line.

82
00:04:36.822 --> 00:04:39.019
The node it ran on gets stamped too.

83
00:04:39.233 --> 00:04:41.175
One more setting, merge_log.

84
00:04:41.175 --> 00:04:44.782
If the message is JSON, its keys become real fields.

85
00:04:44.782 --> 00:04:49.707
And keep_log off drops the original string, so nothing is stored twice.

86
00:04:49.933 --> 00:04:52.172
Now every line says who wrote it.

87
00:04:52.172 --> 00:05:02.960
In Kibana, those stamps become filters you'll use every day: only namespace hello, only app hello-devops, or only one node, to see if one floor is the problem.

88
00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:05.805
A few honest limits.

89
00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:08.548
The front desk is the part that can hurt.

90
00:05:08.548 --> 00:05:13.098
A thousand nodes asking about pods adds real load to the API server.

91
00:05:13.098 --> 00:05:17.715
The filter's use_kubelet option asks each node's own kubelet instead.

92
00:05:17.715 --> 00:05:20.927
Our three-node practice cluster doesn't need it.

93
00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:22.793
A chatty pod can opt out.

94
00:05:22.793 --> 00:05:28.635
With the exclude setting on, an annotation on the pod drops its lines right at the node.

95
00:05:28.867 --> 00:05:32.307
And on first start, Fluent Bit reads new lines only.

96
00:05:32.307 --> 00:05:35.813
So send fresh traffic before you go looking for logs.

97
00:05:37.833 --> 00:05:39.491
So the moral of the story.

98
00:05:39.491 --> 00:05:46.120
One clerk per floor, reading that floor's own folder, and stamping every note with the bed it came from.

99
00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.861
One clerk per floor, a stamp on every note.

100
00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:50.008
A quick check.

101
00:05:50.008 --> 00:05:52.161
A fourth node joins the cluster.

102
00:05:52.161 --> 00:05:55.996
How many Fluent Bit pods now, and who starts the new one?

103
00:05:55.996 --> 00:05:57.072
Pause and think.

104
00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:00.661
Four.

105
00:06:00.661 --> 00:06:02.105
And nobody hires it.

106
00:06:02.105 --> 00:06:07.808
The DaemonSet sees a new floor, and Kubernetes starts a clerk there, by itself.

107
00:06:08.033 --> 00:06:13.777
The lesson's other worry, shelves that never empty, gets a rule for old logs to leave.

108
00:06:13.777 --> 00:06:17.251
And next time, one request crosses several services.

109
00:06:17.251 --> 00:06:18.720
Where did its time go?
